<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Zaragoza</h1><p class="st-meta">1018 CE – 1110 CE (approx.) · capital Zaragoza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The powerful taifa of the Ebro valley whose Hudid kings built the Aljafería palace, employed El Cid and held off Christian advance until 1110.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>, came under the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tujibids">Tujibid</a> governor Mundhir I around 1018. In 1039 the Tujibids were overthrown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-hud">Sulayman ibn Hud al-Mustain</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Hudid dynasty</a>, which went on to rule for some seventy years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">Ahmad I al-Muqtadir</a> (1046-1081) annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aljaferia">Aljafería</a> palace. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-mutaman-ibn-hud">Yusuf al-Mutaman</a> wrote a major treatise on geometry. The kingdom paid tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and from 1081 employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> against Aragon. Its farmland, trade and learning made it one of the richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mustain-ii">Ahmad II al-Mustain</a> was killed fighting Aragon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valtierra">Valtierra</a> in 1110, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. His son held on at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rueda-de-jalon-hudid">Rueda de Jalón (Hudid)</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> captured Zaragoza in 1118.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-zaragoza.jpg" alt="Taifa of Zaragoza" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Zaragoza.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1018 CE – 1110 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaragoza</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sulayman ibn Hud al-Mustain, Ahmad I al-Muqtadir, Yusuf al-Mutaman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Saraqusta, Hudid Kingdom of Zaragoza</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1064&amp;focus=taifa-of-zaragoza" aria-label="Taifa of Zaragoza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4659 693 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Zaragoza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4979 949L4960 959M4979 949L5003 1040M4979 949L5015 951M4979 949L4954 975M4979 949L5013 976M4979 949L4881 1071M4979 949L4799 1289M4979 949L4969 950"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4960 959h0M5003 1040h0M5015 951h0M4954 975h0M5013 976h0M4881 1071h0M4799 1289h0M4969 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4979 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4979 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1064&amp;focus=taifa-of-zaragoza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
